/** @mainpage

This suite exercises tsf-cv - computer vision from the Test Agent it
runs on - against OpenCV, on deterministic images the suite draws on the
agent itself:

- @ref cv-info - an image's dimensions, channels, depth and container;
- @ref cv-compare - a pixel diff of two images: identical copies report
  no change and SSIM 1, and a one-rectangle change is found and boxed;
- @ref cv-match - finding a template cropped from the scene exactly
  where it came from, and not finding a template that is not there;
- @ref cv-region - the colour of a region: the green "indicator" is
  green and the red block is not;
- @ref cv-ocr - recognising a rendered string (skipped when the agent
  has no tesseract);
- @ref cv-decode - decoding a known QR payload (skipped when the agent's
  OpenCV cannot encode the fixture).

There is no key and no network: OpenCV is a library linked into the
agent's RPC server and called in-process, so the image never leaves the
agent. Each test generates its own fixtures with a cv2/numpy script run
on the agent, so the scene - its geometry, colours, text and QR payload -
is known exactly and asserted against. Numeric results (changed pixels
over tolerance, match score by method, green share by region, OCR
confidence) are emitted as MI measurement artifacts, so the HTML log and
Bublik draw them as graphs. The point of the suite is that tsf-cv drives
each OpenCV operation over the RPC and reads the result back as
structured data.

*/
